    </p>
  </Notes>
</TwoColumnSlide>

<Slide kicker="Example 2: LumiLab, a pipeline" title="Measuring glare from ordinary photos">
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Glare:</strong> light bright enough to make seeing
      uncomfortable. Measuring it takes a luminance camera, which few can
      afford
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      <strong>What goes in:</strong> the same scene photographed several
      times, dark to bright, with an ordinary camera, plus that camera's
      calibration files
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      <strong>What comes out:</strong> a luminance map, an image where every
      pixel holds how bright that spot was, in measured units
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      <strong>Who uses it:</strong> lighting designers and daylighting
      researchers
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="caption">
    Repository: <a href={lumiLab.repo.href}>{lumiLab.repo.text}</a><br />
    Project page: <a href={lumiLab.page.href}>{lumiLab.page.text}</a>
  </p>
  <Notes time="0:45" cue="Ask: is there a user clicking through anything here?">
    <p>
      Luminance is how bright a surface looks, measured in candela per
      square meter. The technique is high dynamic range imaging: several
      exposures of one scene, merged and corrected step by step, give the
      same measurement as the expensive camera. The steps come from the
      tutorial the repository's README cites, Pierson, Cauwerts, Bodart, and
      Wienold, "Tutorial: Luminance Maps for Daylighting Studies from High
      Dynamic Range Photography" (<a
        href="https://doi.org/10.1080/15502724.2019.1684319"
        >doi.org/10.1080/15502724.2019.1684319</a
      >), and doing them by hand takes command-line tools and some
      programming.
    </p>
    <p>
      A previous capstone team had built a first desktop app for it. The
      advertised project was to improve that app, "we expect refactoring",
      with installers for Windows and macOS and documentation. The user
      clicks one button; the hard part is what happens to the photos after
      that, so the backbone on the next slide follows the photos.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Example 2: LumiLab, the map" title="One set of photos on its way to a luminance map" wide>
  <StoryMap
    backbone={[
      "Pick the photos",
      "Merge into one image",
      "Crop and resize",
      "Correct the lens",
      "Calibrate the brightness",
      "Save the map",
    ]}
    releases={[
      {
        name: "Walking skeleton",
        stories: [
          "Choose photos in the app",
          "Merge with one camera's settings",
          "Fixed numbers for one lens",
          "Fisheye and dark-edge fixes",
          "Pass it through unchanged",
          "Write one .hdr file",
        ],
      },
      {
        name: "Release 1",
        stories: [
          undefined,
          "Load any camera's response file",
          "Type the numbers in the app",
          "Load the lens calibration files",
          ["Correct for a lens filter", "? Scale to a meter reading"],
          "Record the settings in the file",
        ],
      },
    ]}
  />
  <p class="caption">
    Inferred from the <a href={lumiLab.page.href}>project page</a> and the{" "}
    <a href={lumiLab.repo.href}>repository</a>, not the team's own map.
  </p>
  <Notes time="0:45" cue="Point at Pass it through unchanged: a stub is still a story.">
    <p>
      The columns are the steps of the published technique, in order.
      Merging also resets the exposure, so the image starts from a known
      brightness. Correcting the lens means two fixes: undoing the fisheye's
      distortion, and brightening the edges a lens darkens.
    </p>
    <p>
      The walking skeleton runs one real set of photos through every column
      with the settings fixed for one camera and lens, and lets the
      calibration column pass the image through untouched. The amber card is
      the open question: which reference reading the scale comes from is
      the partner's call, not the team's.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Example 2: LumiLab, the walking skeleton" title="The whole path by week 9, then the rest">
  <ol>
    <li>Fall week 6: the app runs its first pipeline command</li>
    <li class="fragment">Week 7: merge, crop, and resize each work, with fixed settings</li>
    <li class="fragment">Week 8: the two lens fixes</li>
    <li class="fragment">
      Week 9: one button sends the chosen photos through six stages; every
      other setting is still fixed in the code
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      Week 10: the filter, calibration, and file-header stages, and a form
      for the settings
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p class="caption">
    Repository history: <a href={lumiLab.repo.href}>{lumiLab.repo.text}</a>
  </p>
