<Slide kicker="Example 3: TaxonBodyMassML, a research chain" title="Estimating a species' weight from its name">
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The project:</strong> a model that estimates how much a species
      weighs from its scientific name and its relatives
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      <strong>Who uses the result:</strong> ecologists, who need the weight of
      species nobody has weighed
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      <strong>What goes in:</strong> one species name, or a list of them
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      <strong>What comes out:</strong> a weight estimate, where it came from,
      and how sure the model is
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="caption">
    Repository: <a href={taxon.repo.href}>{taxon.repo.text}</a><br />
    Project page: <a href={taxon.page.href}>{taxon.page.text}</a>
  </p>
  <Notes time="0:40" cue="Ask: what would tell this team its model works?">
    <p>
      Ecologists treat body mass as a master trait: it predicts metabolism,
      lifespan, and who eats whom. Thousands of species have been weighed,
      a small share of all described species, so the project predicts the
      rest from the scientific name and the species' place in the tree of
      life. The page asks for an estimate with a provenance flag and an
      uncertainty descriptor, and a web page for single and batch queries.
    </p>
    <p>
      On the map, the baseline is the simplest guess worth beating, the
      method is the model, and the evaluation is how wrong it is on species
      kept aside.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

